test_vma — VMA module selftest
==============================

Phase 1 chunk 1: validates the kernel's VMA red-black tree
implementation (`oskit/kernel/vma.lsysl`) end-to-end via the
debug syscall `SYS_VMA_SELFTEST` (85). The kernel runs the
selftest in its own context, exercises insert / point-lookup /
split / remove / clear-all on a fresh VMATree using the global
pool as scratch space, and returns 0 on pass or `1..N` for the
failure-step code.

Userspace can't yet exercise the VMA API directly — `mmap` /
`munmap` / `mprotect` land in chunk 4. This test exists so
chunks 2 and 3 (page-fault routing, demand paging) have a
known-good baseline to lean on.

Pass criterion: prints `vma: ok`.

    module oskit.bin.test_vma

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, syscall}


    val SYS_VMA_SELFTEST = 86


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("vma: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    main(argv: []string)
        val rc = int(syscall(SYS_VMA_SELFTEST, i64(0)))
        if rc != 0
            fail(rc)
            return
        puts("vma: ok\n")
        exit()
